anly: Opus 5.5 averaged 5.1 points lost per move and the 10k 4.8, with seven blunders each. Opus 5.5 held a lead of +28.5 after move 36 and then gave it back in one move, 37 A3, which lost 37.9 points. The lower-left corner was settled for White from there.", "moments": [{"turn": 12, "text": "White's D9 lost 16.7 points and turned Black's -7.5 into +9.3. Black had a lead for the first time in a while, and a wall was building at D7 and C8."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "Black answered with G6, which lost 15.8 points and dropped the lead from +9.3 to -6.5. The engine's choice was D3. White's H6 then also lost 14.0, so the advantage kept changing hands."}, {"turn": 22, "text": "White's E4 lost 28.6 points and put Black at +16.0. It is one of the biggest swings of the game. Black's D6 lost 23.0 straight away and the lead went to -7.0."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "White's F1 lost 31.6 points and pushed Black's lead to +21.9. With Black stones on G2, F3 and G3 and White's F2 and E2 stones nearby, this was the 10k's worst move. B3 was the engine's choice."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "Opus 5.5 was at +28.5 and played A3, which lost 37.9 points and ended the game as a contest. The lead went from +28.5 to -9.4. The engine's choice was A2. After White took A2 the lower-left corner stayed White's, and Black never got closer than -4.3."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only flood-fills groups and lists their liberties, and count.py only tallies stones plus empty regions bordering a single colour on the pasted current board; the other commands just appended notes to games.md. Nothing loops over candidate moves or plays continuations forward, so neither script chooses or ranks moves.", "stop": false}}